      "writtenOn": "2023-10-24T19:55:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is a new configuration option and should be moved to a separate CL.  There needs be a reason provided for adding this option.\n\nAlso. the comment implies that this option sends a host event, but does not do a reboot.  It actually does both, correct?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 399,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 401,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "ba0aa4c6611e526f4e16aa77607f2a4170fe1f22",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a0d86c51_1f2dd9f5",
        "filename": "include/config.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 401,
      "author": {
        "id": 1442602
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-25T13:48:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The idea with refactoring this part of code was to put \"for event\" under a separate config and disable it, as I don\u0027t see a good reason to send event form EC to AP, when AP-hang is detected (simply the AP is dead from EC perspective). \nThe more I look at this code the more I\u0027m in favor to remove all \"for event\" part, especially that originally the old EC watchdog was pinged by every single host command (with a call to `hang_detect_stop_on_host_command()`) and with this new approach, the watchdog will be kicked by the dedicated message (re-use of EC_HANG_START_NOW). Also the suspend/resume uses cases will be handled on the kernel side.\nTL;DR: Remove code under CONFIG_AP_HANG_DETECT_FOR_EVENT.\nWhat do you think Keith?",
      "parentUuid": "320279e7_01ec7b6e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 399,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 401,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "ba0aa4c6611e526f4e16aa77607f2a4170fe1f22",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
